<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lacquer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lacquerware</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 7000 BCE</span> · Also known as Lacquer, Urushi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">Lacquerware</a> consists of objects coated with lacquer, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> an urushiol-based lacquer from the dried sap of the lacquer tree, Toxicodendron vernicifluum. The oldest known lacquerware, burial ornaments from an Initial Jōmon pit grave at the Kakinoshima site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, is about 9,000 years old, and the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is a red wooden bowl from a Hemudu culture site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. Chinese lacquer became a decorative art under the Shang dynasty and a commercial industry in the state of Chu under the Eastern Zhou, and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Japan; the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> arose independently.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lacquer.jpg" alt="Lacquerware" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liubomir G · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Музей_азійського_мистецтва9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">Lacquerware</a> includes containers, tableware, small objects carried by people and larger pieces such as furniture and even coffins. Before lacquering, a surface may be painted, carved or inlaid with shell, and the lacquer can be dusted with gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Other lacquers come from other plants and from insects: Burmese lacquer is the sap of the thitsee tree, straw-coloured until it turns black in the air, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> shellac, the secretion of the lac bug, has been used since ancient times, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> devotes a chapter to it. European imitations such as Japanning are loosely called imitation lacquer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> set up special administrations to organise lacquer production, and under the Tang craftsmen fixed cut-out sheets of gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to the surface, covered them and ground the layers back, a technique known as pingtuo; carving lacquer also began then. After the 10th century carved lacquer (diaoqi), engraved gold (qiangjin) and filled-in work developed, and inlay with mother-of-pearl flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. Dry lacquer, sculpture built up from lacquer-soaked cloth, was used especially for Buddhist statues.</p><p>Lacquer arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by way of the Korean Peninsula by the 8th century, although experts are divided on whether the older Jōmon lacquer was invented independently. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> the maki-e techniques were developed, in which gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> powder is sprinkled onto designs drawn in lacquer. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> the craft of mother-of-pearl inlay, najeonchilgi, reached its height under the Goryeo dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ming and Qing rulers called Japanese work foreign lacquer, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> took a strong interest in it. From the Azuchi-Momoyama period Japanese lacquer reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> collected it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> the finer ware is thought to have come with craftsmen brought back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>'s conquests of 1555–1562. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, Barniz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasto">Pasto</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and Mexican lacquer, made in the pre-Hispanic period from insect larvae or chia oil, arose independently, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> art school of the 1930s turned Vietnamese lacquer into a painting style, sơn mài.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lacquerware” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 7000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Kakinoshima, Hakodate, Hokkaido, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Zhejiang, China (Hemudu culture) (5th millennium BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Kakinoshima pieces are also dated to the 9th millennium BCE, and a lacquer tree of the Jōmon period is 12,600 years old. Experts are divided on whether Jōmon lacquer was invented independently or derived from Chinese techniques.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=lacquer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
